WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.829 --> 00:00:04.893
The drawing gives the nominal size: the target value of a dimension.

2
00:00:05.591 --> 00:00:09.736
Ø20 means something round, 20 millimetres across.

3
00:00:10.731 --> 00:00:14.423
The tolerance says how far the part may deviate and still work:

4
00:00:14.597 --> 00:00:16.826
here plus or minus five hundredths,

5
00:00:16.988 --> 00:00:19.078
a band of one tenth of a millimetre.

6
00:00:20.116 --> 00:00:27.140
That gives the two spec limits: the lower limit 19.95 and the upper limit 20.05.

7
00:00:27.823 --> 00:00:30.447
The customer accepts anything in between.

8
00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:33.476
A tolerance can also be uneven.

9
00:00:34.192 --> 00:00:37.269
A bore of 12, zero to plus eighteen microns,

10
00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:42.784
runs from exactly twelve to 12.018: bigger is allowed, smaller never.

11
00:00:44.088 --> 00:00:47.037
Dimensions without a tolerance of their own fall

12
00:00:47.095 --> 00:00:49.696
under the general tolerance in the title block,

13
00:00:49.835 --> 00:00:51.240
bottom right on the drawing.

14
00:00:52.245 --> 00:00:55.206
Often that is ISO 2768,

15
00:00:55.391 --> 00:01:01.753
class m: plus or minus 0.2 millimetres for sizes over 6 up to 30 millimetres.

16
00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:06.570
Tighter costs money: a better machine, slower cutting, more measuring.

17
00:01:07.296 --> 00:01:11.313
A bearing seat may need plus or minus 0.01,

18
00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.505
a hole in a cover plate is fine with two tenths.

19
00:01:15.489 --> 00:01:17.556
So ask what the function needs.

20
00:01:18.268 --> 00:01:21.635
An extra decimal to be safe is paid for by production.

21
00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:24.929
A batch of 500 bushes.

22
00:01:25.671 --> 00:01:30.768
In 15 of them the bore measures 12.021: three microns too big.

23
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.485
That is 3 percent out of spec.

24
00:01:34.488 --> 00:01:39.423
Such a part is nonconforming: scrap it, rework it, or use it as it stands,

25
00:01:39.585 --> 00:01:40.769
but only with permission.

26
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.103
That permission is called a deviation,

27
00:01:44.288 --> 00:01:46.889
or a concession: the customer’s written approval.

28
00:01:47.888 --> 00:01:53.264
Found at the machine: 15 times 4 euros 50, so 67 euros 50.

29
00:01:53.999 --> 00:02:01.035
At the customer: €400 to sort everything, €250 in complaint costs and an 8D.

30
00:02:01.708 --> 00:02:02.973
Ten times as much.

31
00:02:04.316 --> 00:02:06.034
No two shafts are the same.

32
00:02:06.778 --> 00:02:11.411
Each measured shaft drops into the lane of its size, and a pile grows.

33
00:02:12.456 --> 00:02:14.372
Two numbers describe that spread.

34
00:02:15.106 --> 00:02:20.818
The mean says where the process sits: for these 25 shafts 20.003.

35
00:02:21.794 --> 00:02:23.199
The standard deviation,

36
00:02:23.338 --> 00:02:29.724
sigma, is the typical distance of a shaft from the mean: here 0.008 millimetres.

37
00:02:30.763 --> 00:02:33.422
Many measurements follow a normal distribution,

38
00:02:33.607 --> 00:02:40.689
the bell curve: 68 percent within one sigma, 95 within two, 99.7 within three.

39
00:02:41.367 --> 00:02:47.335
So nearly everything lies between 19.979 and 20.027.

40
00:02:48.621 --> 00:02:52.917
Spread with a common cause comes from many small sources that are always there:

41
00:02:53.045 --> 00:02:53.672
material,

42
00:02:53.811 --> 00:02:54.821
temperature, play.

43
00:02:55.801 --> 00:02:58.019
That spread is stable and predictable.

44
00:02:58.743 --> 00:03:03.132
You only bring it down by changing the system: machine, fixture or method.

45
00:03:04.185 --> 00:03:06.565
A special cause is a specific event.

46
00:03:07.335 --> 00:03:15.416
On Monday at six the first shafts average 20.025, at seven they are back at 20.005.

47
00:03:16.408 --> 00:03:18.730
The machine was cold after the weekend.

48
00:03:19.475 --> 00:03:23.376
The fix: a 20-minute warm-up, not turning the offsets.

49
00:03:24.687 --> 00:03:27.451
An operator who means well turns the offset

50
00:03:27.544 --> 00:03:30.527
after every shaft by that shaft’s deviation.

51
00:03:31.558 --> 00:03:34.193
That is called tampering, chasing noise.

52
00:03:34.919 --> 00:03:40.410
Each deviation is mostly chance, so correcting for it adds a second noise on top.

53
00:03:41.443 --> 00:03:44.671
The stacks get wider: about 40 percent more spread.

54
00:03:45.383 --> 00:03:47.090
The opposite of what he wanted.

55
00:03:48.124 --> 00:03:53.058
So ask first: did something change, or is this just how wide the process is?

56
00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.112
Does the process fit the tolerance?

57
00:03:56.853 --> 00:04:00.498
That is capability: the spread set beside the tolerance width.

58
00:04:01.538 --> 00:04:09.386
A bore of 30, zero to plus four hundredths: limits 30.000 and 30.040.

59
00:04:10.056 --> 00:04:15.850
Fifty parts give a mean of 30.026, sigma 0.004.

60
00:04:16.859 --> 00:04:20.110
Cp is the tolerance width divided by six sigma,

61
00:04:20.238 --> 00:04:23.059
because six sigma holds nearly all the parts.

62
00:04:23.779 --> 00:04:29.944
0.040 divided by 0.024 is 1.67.

63
00:04:30.942 --> 00:04:35.552
Cp looks only at the width, as if the process sat exactly in the middle.

64
00:04:36.848 --> 00:04:41.237
Cpk counts the centre too: the distance from the mean to the nearest limit,

65
00:04:41.376 --> 00:04:42.897
divided by three sigma.

66
00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:53.243
From 30.026 to the upper limit is fourteen thousandths, divided by 0.012: Cpk 1.17.

67
00:04:53.935 --> 00:04:55.572
Too close to the upper limit.

68
00:04:56.613 --> 00:05:05.007
One offset of minus 0.006 puts the mean at 30.020: Cpk 1.67 too.

69
00:05:05.736 --> 00:05:07.779
No investment, just centring.

70
00:05:08.779 --> 00:05:15.699
The usual minimum is 1.33: centred, about 63 ppm, parts per million, out of spec.

71
00:05:16.452 --> 00:05:18.786
At 1.0 about 2,700.

72
00:05:20.087 --> 00:05:22.572
Cp and Cpk use the short term,

73
00:05:22.804 --> 00:05:26.403
the spread within small groups of parts made one after another.

74
00:05:27.429 --> 00:05:33.420
Ppk uses the overall spread of all parts, including drift over hours and days.

75
00:05:34.121 --> 00:05:35.688
Much lower than the Cpk?

76
00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:37.880
Then the process wanders.

77
00:05:38.862 --> 00:05:44.017
For a launch study customers often ask for a Ppk of at least 1.67,

78
00:05:44.249 --> 00:05:46.095
and only from a stable process.

79
00:05:47.151 --> 00:05:49.531
A Cpk from thirty parts says little.

80
00:05:50.253 --> 00:05:54.003
Take 100 parts or more, in 25 subgroups.

81
00:05:54.705 --> 00:05:57.549
Ask: how many parts, over how many days?
